# Security in Computing Practical

## Practical 2: Configure AAA Authentication on Cisco Router

---

## Aim

To configure Local AAA authentication on a Cisco router for console and VTY (SSH) access.

---

## Topology

• 1 Router (R1)
• 2 PCs (PC0 and PC1)
• All devices connected through LAN

---

## Addressing Table

| Device | Interface | IP Address  | Subnet Mask   | Default Gateway |
| ------ | --------- | ----------- | ------------- | --------------- |
| R1     | Gig0/0    | 192.168.1.1 | 255.255.255.0 | N/A             |
| PC0    | NIC       | 192.168.1.2 | 255.255.255.0 | 192.168.1.1     |
| PC1    | NIC       | 192.168.1.3 | 255.255.255.0 | 192.168.1.1     |

---

## Objectives

1. Configure a local user account on router R1.
2. Configure AAA authentication for console access.
3. Configure AAA authentication for VTY lines using SSH.
4. Verify authentication from console and remote PCs.

---

# Part 1: Basic Router Configuration

### Step 1: Enter Global Configuration Mode

```
R1> enable
R1# configure terminal
```

### Step 2: Configure Interface IP Address

```
R1(config)# interface gigabitEthernet0/0
R1(config-if)# ip address 192.168.1.1 255.255.255.0
R1(config-if)# no shutdown
R1(config-if)# exit
```

### Step 3: Configure Enable Secret Password

```
R1(config)# enable secret enpa55
```

---

# Part 2: Configure Local User Database

### Step 1: Create Local Username and Password

```
R1(config)# username admin privilege 15 secret adminpa55
```

---

# Part 3: Configure AAA for Console Login

### Step 1: Enable AAA Service

```
R1(config)# aaa new-model
```

### Step 2: Create Default AAA Authentication Method

```
R1(config)# aaa authentication login default local
```

### Step 3: Apply AAA Authentication to Console Line

```
R1(config)# line console 0
R1(config-line)# login authentication default
R1(config-line)# exit
```

### Step 4: Exit Configuration Mode

```
R1(config)# end
```

### Step 5: Verify Console Login

System will prompt for:

Username: admin
Password: adminpa55

---

# Part 4: Configure AAA for VTY Lines (SSH Access)

### Step 1: Configure Domain Name

```
R1(config)# ip domain-name ccnasecurity.com
```

### Step 2: Generate RSA Keys

```
R1(config)# crypto key generate rsa general-keys modulus 1024
```

### Step 3: Enable SSH Version 2

```
R1(config)# ip ssh version 2
```

### Step 4: Create Named AAA Authentication List for SSH

```
R1(config)# aaa authentication login SSH-LOGIN local
```

### Step 5: Configure VTY Lines to Use AAA

```
R1(config)# line vty 0 4
R1(config-line)# login authentication SSH-LOGIN
R1(config-line)# transport input ssh
R1(config-line)# exit
```

### Step 6: Save Configuration

```
R1# write memory
```

---

# Part 5: Verification

## Step 1: Test Network Connectivity

From PC0:

```
ping 192.168.1.3
```

From PC1:

```
ping 192.168.1.2
```

Both pings should be successful.

---

## Step 2: Test SSH Login

From PC0 Command Prompt:

```
ssh -l admin 192.168.1.1
```

From PC1 Command Prompt:

```
ssh -l admin 192.168.1.1
```

When prompted:

Username: admin
Password: adminpa55

Router prompt should appear:

```
R1>
```

---

# Result

Local AAA authentication for console and SSH access has been successfully configured and verified.

---

# Viva Questions

1. What is AAA?
   AAA stands for Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting. It is used to control user access to network devices.

2. What is the purpose of AAA new-model?
   It enables AAA security services on the router.

3. Why is SSH preferred over Telnet?
   SSH provides encrypted communication, while Telnet sends data in plain text.

4. What is the use of RSA keys in SSH?
   RSA keys are used for secure encrypted communication between devices.

5. What is the difference between local authentication and line password?
   Local authentication uses usernames and encrypted passwords stored in the router database, while line passwords use a single shared password.

---

# Precautions

• Always remember login credentials.
• Use strong passwords.
• Save configuration after completing settings.
• Use SSH instead of Telnet for secure access.

